1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
 How does architectural knowledge safely change over time?

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,400
 Hello, I'm Russell and welcome to episode 14 of the Articulate Journal,

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:15,000
 where I'm exploring what it takes to build an AI native architecture from first principles.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 Earlier in the journal, I introduced a knowledge model and the idea of representing architectural knowledge as claims.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 A claim is an assertion about an architecture.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 The order service uses Postgres. A customer API runs in AWS.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 The CRM platform is owned by the customer experience team.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 But these aren't necessarily absolute truths.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 They are things Articulate currently has some reason to believe.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 And that creates the question for this episode.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 What happens when new information arrives?

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 One of the goals for Articulate is to create a living architectural model.

13
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 Architectural information might come from a discovery conversation,

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 an architecture decision record, solution design, source code, or from the architect directly,

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 or eventually from agents reasoning over information Articulate already knows.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 If the knowledge model represents Articulate's understanding of an architecture,

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 now understanding has to evolve as new information appears.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 But there's a tension here.

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 If the knowledge model cannot change, it becomes stale.

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 But if anything can change it freely, it stops being trustworthy.

21
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 So the problem isn't really how do we update architectural knowledge.

22
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 It's how do we safely evolve what Articulate understands about an architecture?

23
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 Imagine Articulate currently believes the order service uses Postgres.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 There is evidence supporting that claim.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 And we have reasonably high confidence in that.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 Then some new information arrives.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 The order service uses MongoDB.

28
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
 So what should Articulate do?

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 It can't simply replace Postgres with Mongo.

30
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 Perhaps Mongo describes the future migration.

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 Perhaps the service currently uses both.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 Perhaps the new information is wrong.

33
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 Or perhaps Postgres was correct six months ago and Mongo is correct today.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 That's an important distinction.

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 New information isn't automatically new truth.

36
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 It has to be understood in a context of what Articulate already knows.

37
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 And that led me to the idea of proposed knowledge.

38
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 New information doesn't directly modify the authoritative knowledge model.

39
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
 Instead, it enters a process.

40
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 Something like this.

41
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 Incoming information initially becomes proposed knowledge.

42
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 That proposed knowledge is interpreted and reconciled before becoming accepted knowledge.

43
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 The exact implementation isn't important here.

44
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 What matters are the responsibilities.

45
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 Articulate first needs to understand what the new information is actually saying.

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 Then it needs to reconcile that information with what it already knows.

47
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 Does it support something we already believe?

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 Or does it contradict it?

49
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 Does it supersede something that was already previously true?

50
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 Does it describe a future state?

51
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 Or do we simply not have enough evidence to know yet?

52
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 Sometimes the correct answer will be we don't know.

53
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 That might require more evidence.

54
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 It might trigger further discovery, it might require an architect to make that judgement.

55
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 I don't want an agent manufacturing architectural certainty simply because the underlying data model expects one answer.

56
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 Uncertainty is a part of architectural knowledge.

57
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 Time becomes particularly important during reconciliation.

58
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 We might eventually understand that the order service used Postgres,

59
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 the order service currently uses Mongo and the order service might move to Dynamo.

60
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 Those aren't contradictory claims.

61
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 Together they describe the evolution of the architecture of once was and as is and what is to be.

62
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 Importantly, accepting Mongo doesn't mean deleting Postgres.

63
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 If Postgres generally represents the architecture previously, it remains part of the architectural history.

64
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
 This matters because architectural decisions have contexts.

65
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 A decision made two years ago should be understood against the architecture that existed two years ago.

66
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 Something that looks like a poor decision today may have been completely reasonable given the constraints knowledge and the architecture that existed at the time.

67
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 So the knowledge hasn't simply been corrected.

68
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
 Our understanding has evolved.

69
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 This is where architectural baselines become important.

70
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 Understanding the baseline architecture is something that architects say a lot.

71
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 Without articulating, I think a baseline gives a phrase a specific meaning.

72
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 A baseline represents an accepted architectural viewpoint or a particular point in the architecture's evolution.

73
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 It doesn't need to duplicate the entire knowledge model.

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
 The knowledge model remains one connected body of architectural knowledge.

75
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 The baseline determines the viewpoint through which that knowledge is understood.

76
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 A current baseline represents the architecture we understand to exist today.

77
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 A previous baseline allows us to understand the architecture as it once was.

78
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 And a target baseline can represent the architecture we're intending to create.

79
00:05:51,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 This gives us that once was, as is and to be states, without maintaining three disconnected architecture models.

80
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 And because the underlying claims, evidence and decisions remain connected, we can ask more than what did the architecture look like.

81
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
 We can also ask, why did it look like that?

82
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
 Or what changed between these baselines?

83
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
 That's much closer to how I want to articulate to understand architectural change.

84
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 There's another consequence of this model.

85
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
 Proposed knowledge might remain proposed for quite some time.

86
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
 Imagine a significant architectural proposal that requires human approval.

87
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 The proposal was created and reconciled against the current baseline.

88
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 It looks reasonable, but the architect doesn't approve it till two weeks later.

89
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 During those two weeks, the architecture may have changed.

90
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 Another decision may have been accepted.

91
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 Dependency may have changed.

92
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
 A system may now be scheduled for assignment.

93
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
 New evidence may have appeared.

94
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 The proposal was valid against the baseline from two weeks ago.

95
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
 That doesn't necessarily mean it's valid against the current baseline.

96
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 So approval alone isn't necessary enough to make something accepted as architectural knowledge.

97
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:22,000
 Before the proposal was merged, it may need to be reconciled against the latest baseline.

98
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:27,000
 I think this is particularly interesting after the previous episodes on durable execution.

99
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 The execution can pause.

100
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:35,000
 A human can take days to respond, but the architecture doesn't pause while we're waiting.

101
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
 When execution resumes, the world may have changed.

102
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 There's one important distinction. Articulate isn't ultimately going to present architects

103
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
 with thousands of disconnected claims.

104
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 Claims are the assertions underneath the architecture understanding.

105
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 Imagine Articulate has accepted these claims.

106
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
 Order service runs in AWS ECS.

107
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
 Order service reads and writes to the order database.

108
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
 The order database is Postgres SQL, and the order database runs in AWS RDS.

109
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 Individually, these are architecture assertions.

110
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
 Together, they begin to describe a system.

111
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
 They identify architectural entities, they establish relationships,

112
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,000
 and they create a connected architecture model.

113
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,000
 Other claims might describe ownership, business capabilities, characteristics,

114
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 principles, and decisions.

115
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 The architecture emerges from the relationships between what Articulate has learnt.

116
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
 But the underlying claim remains important because they explain why Articulate

117
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
 believes those relationships exist.

118
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 What evidence supports them, where they came from, and how confident we are.

119
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:00,000
 And when they're believed to be true, the architecture model gives us understanding.

120
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
 The claims give us the understanding context.

121
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 This has changed how I think about the knowledge model.

122
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
 Originally, the problem sounded like a data modeling problem.

123
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
 How should Articulate store architectural information?

124
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
 But storage isn't really the interesting problem.

125
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,000
 The interesting problem is maintaining an evolving understanding of the architecture.

126
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
 New information has to be interpreted.

127
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
 It has to be reconciled with the existing understanding.

128
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
 Time has to be considered.

129
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
 Evidence and confidence have to matter.

130
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
 And sometimes, uncertainty has to remain unresolved.

131
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
 This also clarifies an important architectural boundary.

132
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
 Agents shouldn't simply modify the knowledge model.

133
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
 They can contribute information.

134
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,000
 They can interpret it.

135
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
 They can reconcile claims.

136
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
 They can gather evidence.

137
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
 They can reason about what has changed.

138
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 But changing authoritative architectural knowledge is a governed behavior.

139
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 There's not simply a database update.

140
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
 Knowledge evolution is in itself an architectural capability.

141
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,000
 So, we now have a model where Articulate can continually receive new architectural information

142
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
 without automatically treating that information as truth.

143
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,000
 New information becomes proposed knowledge.

144
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
 It is interpreted and reconciled against existing understanding.

145
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 Accepted knowledge can evolve our understanding of the architecture without rewriting what came before it.

146
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:41,000
 And baselines allow us to understand the architecture from different points in its evolution.

147
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 Here's another problem.

148
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 A proposed change can be perfectly consistent with what Articulate already knows

149
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
 and still be a bad architectural change.

150
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
 It might violate an architectural principle.

151
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:00,000
 It might introduce a dependency on a system that we're trying to retire.

152
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:06,000
 It might improve availability while significantly increasing operational complexity.

153
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
 Knowing what the architecture is isn't enough.

154
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
 We also need to evaluate what knowledge means.

155
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
 And that is the question for the next episode.

156
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 How can Articulate continuously evaluate an architecture rather than simply storing it?

157
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 That's where architectural assurance begins.

158
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 See you on the next one.

